#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9f4654-e89d-5d9e-a79c-0bb5f857defd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282101} {:answer/id #uuid "85c51f51-7216-584b-b827-69d84ec4f79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282842} {:answer/id #uuid "beb360a4-ee13-5f31-9c0a-e76bcd78e6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283346} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d331ae-1ad5-5557-89ef-e15bbaae3a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.482" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283667} {:answer/id #uuid "e74c9b4d-a866-5c53-8a6c-93a7e66b72ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.274" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beb360a4-ee13-5f31-9c0a-e76bcd78e6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283346} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff6cf4f-69f4-50e0-b11a-e6431b91e83f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9eb149a3-bdcc-552e-a682-ced670cd4013" :question/math-academy-id "q-167171" :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)$ at the point $({\\pi }^{2},0).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,Δx-\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & ≈f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dad359-25f2-59b1-a326-992f7d8f2be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345374} {:answer/id #uuid "37a0854b-5ffd-53b5-a97e-3fc8c049dafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345828} {:answer/id #uuid "78a766e2-5151-5cd4-bd3b-cdc71e40524c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346407} {:answer/id #uuid "9d887fd2-2962-51ac-ad91-2c074402c2fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346722} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcdcfbb-2ee8-5177-94d0-d8a8b2bddfea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}x+(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a0854b-5ffd-53b5-a97e-3fc8c049dafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345828} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ddc30c-7b7f-5181-82b4-b214c29b463f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af353571-e5f3-5cb8-87db-52892f4fbe38" :question/math-academy-id "q-79739" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{x}^{3}y-{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009c9900-f889-52f9-b00b-40100c9397c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345359} {:answer/id #uuid "33de2cd4-781a-5e11-9d06-401da624c0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345803} {:answer/id #uuid "6c775b28-e659-58c3-afb4-970c5a4d0320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{d}a+24\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346292} {:answer/id #uuid "80dabf23-d4b2-59f9-93af-a8e0472606a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,\\text{d}a+48\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346477} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0dac21-38b0-572f-851b-fdb97e5e86a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{d}a+24\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009c9900-f889-52f9-b00b-40100c9397c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345359} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0653cbc-f79b-58c9-bc1b-48709188cc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f0e792d-8d7d-5fba-9784-ec8ac9d90e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-79747" :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the surface area $S$ of the rectangular solid if the base is a square with the side of $a=3$ and the height is $h=7.$" :question/worked-solution "The surface area of the given rectangular solid is $S=2{a}^{2}+4ah.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(a,h) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂S}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =(4a+4h)\\,\\text{d}a+4a\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=3$ and $h=7,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(3,7) & =(4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 7)\\,\\text{d}a+4\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5f0ff3-67c3-5156-80d4-3b1056e54271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345456} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c1f15-75a4-5090-ab69-ed2b1d6b4397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346839} {:answer/id #uuid "b34d8f8b-645c-5063-9782-d98391879677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346918} {:answer/id #uuid "c7663d80-ea3d-53cf-9cff-cd7f8bff08ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347085} {:answer/id #uuid "de53245c-33e1-5dc2-9980-d68bbba16eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de53245c-33e1-5dc2-9980-d68bbba16eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347307} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d29f153-5da4-5bde-bade-0dc96a9e6da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01cbcb4d-5667-5e3e-806c-ef99a5773f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79813" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & ≈-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18aa2d29-4200-55a9-9049-eec28d6ce46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345569} {:answer/id #uuid "28d8915f-3339-5227-a3ee-86c79928eb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345717} {:answer/id #uuid "39be8076-d1a4-53bc-beb7-69d5faf74820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345843} {:answer/id #uuid "e580e49f-e01d-5220-aa00-628de33ae96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347372} {:answer/id #uuid "fb111822-7947-5e96-ad01-3bd29918f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18aa2d29-4200-55a9-9049-eec28d6ce46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345569} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7d86ef-4dea-5ade-8600-60a413066868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a50b343-0303-5110-8cf9-6975a85c04cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79815" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$"}]}
